    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Though the Soviet Union fell decades ago, the red specter of communism still looms \r\nlarge over parts of the world, including Colombia. In this thesis, I explore the anticommunist \r\nrhetoric in Colombia through the discourse of two former presidents who lived in two very \r\ndifferent realities. I compare the discourse of Laureano Gómez with that of Álvaro Uribe Vélez,\r\nusing a speech titled “El Basilisco” as a case study of Gómez’s discourse and various tweets \r\npublished by Uribe between 2016 and 2018 as examples of his discourse. I argue that while \r\ncommunism was a “real” threat during Gómez’s time and within his rhetoric, for Uribe, his \r\nanticommunist discourse function less as an expression of a real threat and more as a tool that\r\nleverages the history and context of Colombia and to a certain extent Venezuela. I place the \r\ndiscourse of both men firmly within the general context that shaped their time, meaning that I \r\nexplore various events that occurred during the 20th century in Colombia, paying particular\r\nattention to the position of Marxism within Colombian society. Furthermore, I also briefly \r\ndiscuss the implications of the evolution of communication from the present day in comparison \r\nto the 1940s and 50s. I also engage with various literature across multiple fields such as history, \r\ncommunication, and political science.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Acevedo, J. (2022). Entre Camaradas: The Transformation of Anticommunist Rhetoric in Colombia Through the Discourse of Laureano Gómez and Álvaro Uribe Vélez. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/dm155pq0226",
            "type": "preferred citation",
